## 2026-08-23 - STEP 19 (Adversary v1 - collision-maximising input)
- what: adversary.py opens Phase P5. Threat model: known-seed / white-box attacker (Crosby & Wallach, "Denial of Service via Algorithmic Complexity Attacks", USENIX Security 2003 - the hash-flooding model) who already knows a CMS instance's exact (width, depth, seed). why: every prior step measured the sketch under RANDOM keys; the eps/delta guarantee is itself a promise about the sketch's OWN hash randomness for a fixed stream - it says nothing about a stream chosen by someone who knows the hash functions. concept: worst-case vs average-case; attacking a probabilistic guarantee under a named, disclosed threat model (not a claim that CMS is broken in general).
- mechanism: find_colliding_group() brute-force-searches deterministic candidates ("{namespace}-{i}") for a group that shares the SAME bucket index in EVERY row simultaneously (a "full-row collision group"). adversarial_stream() builds N disjoint such groups and replays each key events_per_key times; random_control_stream() is the benign comparison (same distinct-key count, same total events, same shuffle) fed into a sketch with identical width/depth/seed (same memory budget).
- gate result (25 seeds, deliberately small demo sketch width=16 depth=3, 5 groups x 5 keys x 20 events/key = 500 events): adversarial stream beat the benign control's mean overestimation error on 25/25 seeds (100% win rate); mean adversarial error 81.60 vs mean control error 11.33 - a 7.2x ratio. Every colliding-group member's estimate is inflated in BOTH rows at once (min-estimator has no clean row left to trust); benign random collisions almost never hit every row simultaneously at this width/depth.
- flip-side (safety) check: against a production-sized sketch (size_cms(0.01, 0.05) = width 272, depth 3), the identical search fails within 5,000 trials with a RuntimeError - the birthday space (272^3 ~ 20M bucket-tuples) dwarfs the search budget. Confirms the attack needs a small and/or known-seed sketch; a correctly-sized deployment with a secret, re-randomized seed is not vulnerable to this construction.
- gate design note: core gate (plan.json step 19): adversarial stream provably raises mean estimation error vs random stream at same memory. 9 new tests cover: collision mechanism verified directly (every group member shares the row-bucket tuple), infeasibility against a production sketch, stream-shape/group-membership correctness, single-seed sanity (>2x error), 25-seed robustness (>=90% win rate + >=1.5x mean-error ratio - both cleared at 100% and 7.2x), and a control-stream sanity check that the benign side isn't secretly adversarial. 191 total green.
- Commit 3352e4a, CI green 59s. Phase P5 (Adversarial stress) started. Next: step 20, guarantee-violation meter - measure empirical P(error > eps*N) vs the promised delta on benign streams, and quantify it on the step-19 adversarial stream.

## 2026-08-24 - STEP 20 (Guarantee-violation meter)
- what: guarantee_meter.py -- packages the eps/delta bound check into a reusable meter (violating_keys(), violation_rate()) instead of a one-off test, then applies it to two call sites: benign_violation_study() (reruns step 6's 300-hash-seed check as a general utility) and adversarial_violation_study() (the same meter applied to a step-19 collision-maximising stream). why: step 6 checked "does the eps/delta promise hold" once, informally, inside a test. step 19 then broke that same promise with an engineered stream but never measured the break on the SAME scale as the original check. This step closes that gap: one meter, two streams, directly comparable numbers. concept: does the eps/delta promise actually hold, and by how much does a known-seed attacker break it -- turning a theorem into something you can measure and report.
- benign result (eps=0.01, delta=0.05, 300 hash-seed trials, 10k Zipfian stream, top-10 heavy-hitter keys): every key measured 0.0 empirical violation rate across all 300 trials -- comfortably inside the delta=0.05 promise (gate uses the same 3*delta=0.15 sampling-noise margin step 6 established, all keys land far under it).
- adversarial result (step-19 demo sketch: width=16, depth=3, seed=42, 5 collision groups x 5 keys x 20 events/key = 500 events, eps=0.02 -> bound=10): the collision-maximising stream hit a 100% violation rate (1.0) -- every colliding-group member broke the bound in every row simultaneously. The size-matched benign control (same width/depth/seed, same event count, same shuffle) hit 48% (0.48) -- notably nonzero because this demo sketch is deliberately undersized (16x3, not sized via size_cms) to make step 19's attack tractable, so even ordinary collisions strain it; the adversarial stream is still decisively worse (~2x the violation rate, and every single group member fails vs under half of the benign keys).
- gate design note: core gate (plan.json step 20): "on benign streams empirical violation rate <= delta; on adversarial streams it is quantified." 6 new tests cover: violating_keys/violation_rate mechanics on a hand-built sketch (correct flagging, zero-true-counts edge case, fraction-of-keys correctness), the benign gate (10 keys, 300 trials, all <= 3*delta margin), a smaller benign_violation_study smoke test, and the adversarial gate (meter applied to step-19's adversarial_stream vs random_control_stream, asserting adv_rate > ctl_rate and adv_rate >= 0.5 -- decisive, not marginal). 197 total green.
