## 2026-08-25 - STEP 21 (CU vs plain CMS under adversarial load)
- what: adversarial_study.py -- runs {plain CMS, CU-CMS} x {step-19 adversarial stream, size-matched benign control} across 10 hash seeds at identical (width,depth,seed) memory budgets, measures each cell with the step-20 meter, and ships the comparison as report/adversarial.csv. why: step 9 proved CU cuts overestimation on ordinary streams and step 19 broke plain CMS with full-row collision groups -- this step answers the natural question the two leave open: is conservative update a defense against the attack? concept: which variant is robust, and why -- the core empirical finding of Phase P5. next: step 22, adversary on real traces (replay MAWI/CIC with heavy-hitter amplification, report/real_adversarial.png).
- CORE FINDING: conservative update does NOT survive the attack. Against full-row collision groups CU degenerates into plain CMS: every group member shares the SAME bucket in EVERY row, so CU's add() finds min == all of the group's cells and bumps them in lock-step -- CU's whole trick (trusting some cleaner row) has no cleaner row left to trust. Numbers (10 seeds, demo sketch width=16 depth=3, 5 groups x 5 keys x 20 events, eps=0.02): adversarial stream -> plain CMS violation_rate 1.000 / mean_error 82.00, CU violation_rate 1.000 / mean_error 80.04 (97.6% of plain -- no rescue). Benign control at the same memory -> plain 0.464 / 11.04, CU 0.000 / 2.00 (step 9's advantage intact: zero violations, 5.5x lower error). Robustness against a known-seed attacker comes from sketch sizing + a secret, re-randomized seed (step 19's flip-side check), not from conservative update.
- mechanism notes: both variants share the step-2 HashFamily for identical (width,depth,seed), so a collision group found against one applies verbatim to the other -- that is what makes the comparison apples-to-apples. measure_stream() replays any stream into a fresh sketch and returns violation_rate (step-20 meter) + mean_overestimation_error (step-19 metric). adversarial_cu_study() re-searches collision groups per seed (the attack is seed-specific). write_adversarial_csv() emits the 10-column artifact; `PYTHONPATH=src python -m sketchflow.adversarial_study` regenerates it in one command.
- gate design note: core gate (plan.json step 21): "artifact: report/adversarial.csv comparing violation rates; finding written to decisions.log" -- both delivered. 7 new tests cover: single-seed CU non-rescue (violation 1.0 for both variants, CU error >= 0.9x plain and >= 80), CU's benign edge preserved across 3 seeds (never worse, strictly better in aggregate), attack beats control for BOTH variants on every seed, row shape/bounds, determinism, CSV round-trip, and committed-artifact wellformedness (40 rows, CU failing under attack). 204 total green.
- reconcile note (Rule 2): step-21 code was found complete-but-uncommitted in the working tree (built by a prior run that died before commit -- same pattern as the 12-Aug step-8 reconcile). Code wins: verified all 204 tests green locally, then committed as today's step.


## 2026-08-26 - STEP 22 (Adversary on real traces - heavy-hitter amplification)
- what: real_adversary.py + real_plot.py close Phase P5 by taking the adversarial stress-test onto the two REAL traces (MAWI backbone pcap step 18, CIC-IDS-2017 flows step 16) under a DIFFERENT, weaker, more realistic threat model than steps 19-21. why: steps 19-21 used a known-seed white-box attacker (Crosby and Wallach 2003) picking full-row-colliding keys - decisive but needs a small/leaked-seed sketch and synthetic attacker-chosen keys. Real backbone traffic can't be hand-picked to collide; the realistic real-world attack is VOLUMETRIC - send more traffic to flows that are already heavy (DDoS/amplification). concept: attacking a probabilistic guarantee with volume instead of hash knowledge; the difference between the theorem's self-scaling bound and an operator's fixed sized-for-baseline promise.
- mechanism: heavy_hitter_targets() picks the top-N heaviest real flows; amplify_stream(stream, targets, factor) replays the real trace but multiplies each occurrence of a target by factor (nothing about keys or sketch internals is touched, only volume on existing heavy flows). measure_amplified() judges the amplified stream against TWO thresholds: theorem eps*N (N = live length, self-scaling) and provisioned eps*N0 (N0 = baseline length the operator sized for, FIXED). real_adversarial_study() sweeps factor {1,2,5,10,20,50} x provisioning {eps=0.01 sized w=272, eps=0.1 under-provisioned w=28}, sizing the CMS once via size_cms per level.
- CORE FINDING (which promise breaks, not 'CMS breaks'): a WELL-SIZED sketch is essentially immune to amplification on real traffic - MAWI mean error 22.2 -> 23.0 even at x50; CIC-IDS dead flat 3.34 across all factors (the amplified top ports occupy their own uncontested buckets, so no light flow absorbs their mass); provisioned violation ~0. UNDER-PROVISIONING is what breaks the operator's fixed baseline promise eps*N0: MAWI 0 -> 3.4 percent of flows violate by x20-50; CIC 0.07 -> 2.4 percent by x50; mean error climbs (CIC 49.6 -> 163). The SELF-SCALING textbook bound eps*N HOLDS everywhere (theorem violation rate <= 0.0009 max) because it loosens exactly as fast as N grows - volume alone does not break the theorem. Proven invariant (asserted per row): violation_rate_theorem <= violation_rate_provisioned, since N>=N0 => eps*N>=eps*N0 => the theorem threshold is always >= the provisioned one. Robustness against volumetric amplification comes from provisioning for peak (or re-sizing under load), NOT from the theorem and NOT from conservative update.
- gate design note: core gate (plan.json step 22): 'artifact report/real_adversarial.png; where the bound holds vs breaks on real data' - delivered (+ .csv, 24 rows), regenerable via PYTHONPATH=src python -m sketchflow.real_plot (loads both real traces, runs the study, writes CSV + PNG in one command). 16 new tests cover: heavy-hitter target selection (heaviest keys, n=0/oversized/negative), amplify mechanics (factor-1 identity, only-targets multiplied, factor<1 raises), measure (never-undercount, flat when roomy, theorem<=provisioned invariant), study shape + holds-vs-breaks (sized immune / under-provisioned breaks / error monotone), N grows while baseline fixed, CSV round-trip, PNG regeneration, committed-artifact presence, and a real-MAWI integration test (skips cleanly if dpkt/data absent). 220 total green.
- teach 4-line: WHAT a real-world volumetric attacker (flooding the heaviest flows) does to a Count-Min Sketch on real backbone traffic. WHY it matters: it separates the textbook eps/delta bound (which self-scales with traffic and survives) from the operator's one-time memory sizing (which does not). CONCEPT: self-scaling theorem bound vs fixed provisioned promise; provision for peak, not baseline. NEXT: step 23, write up the whole empirical study (Phase P6).
